# python-activity-based-learning
Case Study #2: Progressive Electricity Billing
Problem Statement
Design a Python program that computes monthly household electricity bills using progressive consumption slabs, a fixed charge, a percentage surcharge, and a low-usage rebate. The program must compare bills across consecutive months and flag any unusual month-over-month cost increase.   
Python Activity1 case studies.pdf

Objectives
Calculate slab-wise energy charges accurately for multi-month consumption data.

Apply fixed charges, surcharges, and rebates under defined conditions.

Detect and report unusual percentage jumps between consecutive monthly bills.

Assumptions
Slab 1: first 100 units @ Rs. 3.00/unit; Slab 2: next 100 units (101–200) @ Rs. 4.50/unit; Slab 3: units above 200 @ Rs. 6.50/unit.

Fixed charge is Rs. 100.00 per month; surcharge is 5% on (energy_charge + fixed_charge).

A Rs. 50.00 rebate applies when monthly consumption is 150 units or fewer.

An increase of more than 30% over the immediately preceding month's bill is flagged as an unusual increase.

Algorithm
Start and store monthly (month, units) tuples in a list along with tariff constants.

For each month, split units across the three consumption slabs to compute energy_charge.

Add fixed_charge, apply the 5% surcharge, subtract the Rs. 50.00 rebate if units <= 150, and round to two decimal places.

Store (month, units, total_bill) in a bills list.

Loop through bills; from index 1 onward, compute percentage change from the previous month and mark "UNUSUAL INCREASE" if above 30%.

Display each month's units, bill amount, and status, then end.

Flowchart
Plaintext
[Start] --> [Load monthly_units & tariff rules]
  --> [Loop: (month, units) in monthly_units]
  --> [Compute slab 1, 2, 3 charges + fixed + 5% surcharge - rebate]
  --> [Append (month, units, total_bill) to bills]
  --> {More months?} --Yes--> [Loop]
  --No--> [Loop index i from 0 to len(bills)-1]
  --> {i > 0 and pct_change > 30%?} --Yes--> [Mark UNUSUAL INCREASE]
  --No--> [Mark Normal]
  --> [Print monthly summary] --> [End]
Pseudocode
Plaintext
START
  SET monthly_units = [("Jan", 120), ("Feb", 135), ("Mar", 260), ("Apr", 140)]
  SET fixed_charge = 100.0, surcharge_rate = 0.05, rebate_low_usage = 50.0, spike_threshold = 0.30
  CREATE empty list bills

  FOR each (month, units) IN monthly_units DO
    SET energy_charge = 0.0, remaining = units
    IF remaining > 0 THEN
      slab1 = MIN(remaining, 100)
      energy_charge = energy_charge + slab1 * 3.0
      remaining = remaining - slab1
    END IF
    IF remaining > 0 THEN
      slab2 = MIN(remaining, 100)
      energy_charge = energy_charge + slab2 * 4.5
      remaining = remaining - slab2
    END IF
    IF remaining > 0 THEN
      energy_charge = energy_charge + remaining * 6.5
    END IF
    subtotal = energy_charge + fixed_charge
    surcharge = subtotal * surcharge_rate
    rebate = rebate_low_usage IF units <= 150 ELSE 0.0
    total_bill = ROUND(subtotal + surcharge - rebate, 2)
    APPEND (month, units, total_bill) TO bills
  END FOR

  FOR i = 0 TO LENGTH(bills) - 1 DO
    SET status = "Normal"
    IF i > 0 AND bills[i-1].total_bill > 0 THEN
      pct_change = (bills[i].total_bill - bills[i-1].total_bill) / bills[i-1].total_bill
      IF pct_change > spike_threshold THEN
        status = "UNUSUAL INCREASE"
      END IF
    END IF
    PRINT bills[i].month, bills[i].units, bills[i].total_bill, status
  END FOR
END
Test Cases
Case ID	Input (month, units)	Expected Behavior / Output
TC1	("Jan", 120)	Slab 1 (300) + Slab 2 (90) + Fixed (100) = 490; +5% (24.5) - 50 rebate = Rs. 464.50, Normal
TC2	("Feb", 135)	Rs. 518.18, Normal (+11.55% over Jan)
TC3	("Mar", 260)	High usage across all 3 slabs, no rebate; flags UNUSUAL INCREASE (>30% jump over Feb)
TC4	("Apr", 140)	Rebate re-applied (units <= 150), bill drops sharply, marked Normal
Conclusion
The program demonstrates slab-based conditional logic, list/tuple storage, and sequential comparison to turn raw meter readings into actionable monthly billing insights. A practical limitation is that tariff slabs and seasonal anomalies are hardcoded rather than loaded from an external file.


